versions.analysis_for_next:
Version 1 overcorrected toward neutral: 59 positive and 49 negative tweets went to neutral. Neutral errors fell, but the gain is smaller than the losses.

Positive tweets the model called neutral fall into three groups. First, friendly promotions and invitations ('See you there!', 'come check it out!', 'Don't miss...', tickets at a 'good price'). Second, personal plans for fun things (Disneyland, homecoming 'should be fun', Dunkin). Third, playful or affectionate chatter with :) or <3.

Negative tweets the model called neutral are mostly mild complaints and frustrations ('Argh', '#boring', 'stuck in the airport', 'poor decision', 'I'm a loser'). Sarcasm marked with #not and dismissive opinions ('most plain wrestler', 'Don't play Nicki') also went to neutral.

Neutral remains right for these cases:
- dry headlines, scores and statistics;
- legal, business or political news without a stated stance;
- tactical sports talk;
- plain questions.

Some bad-news headlines (an arrest, an oil-price crash, Charlie Hebdo) are neutral, so only violence or tragedy should make news negative.

Fix: neutral becomes the label for tweets with no personal affect at all. Any friendly, eager or fun tone leans positive, and any gripe, annoyance or sarcastic dig leans negative. Keep 'only clear celebration or tragedy' for news.

Version 6 is the best so far at 72.7. The remaining errors are mostly neutral tweets: 52 went to negative and 38 to positive.

The neutral tweets called negative are mainly:
- reported conflict, arrests, debates or market news in headline form ('slams Trump', 'arrest of Nabeel Rajab', 'Oil Price Not Done Crashing');
- jokes and dark humor ('scream cheater', 'Batman put people in comas', 'Chuck Norris');
- mild preferences or quitting statements ('doesn't like bdubs', 'I'm done', 'HELL NO' at a price, envy).

The neutral tweets called positive are mainly:
- event ads and hype ('Don't miss the event!', 'place to be', 'COOL OUT goes down', 'Retweet if you're ready', 'keep voting');
- plans with hope ('I'm hoping it does the book justice');
- nostalgia ('oh my god' at an old iPod);
- celebrity human-interest news ('kind words', birthday).

I make small targeted additions rather than rework. Third-person headlines are neutral unless they report violence or death or clear achievements. Event hype without a praise word is neutral. Jokes and dark humor are neutral unless they attack someone. To stay under 400 words, I shorten the cue lists and keep version 6's examples, which anchor the polar gains.

Version 8 (72.2) did slightly worse than version 6 (72.7). Adding 'omg' and excited exclamations to positive pulled a few more neutral tweets to positive (42 vs 38): 'oh my god' at an old iPod, 'Don't miss the event!', 'COOL OUT goes down'. The harmless-jokes clause did not reduce neutral-to-negative errors (48 vs 52). The differences between versions 4, 6 and 8 are within about half a point, so the remaining errors look like label noise or genuinely ambiguous tweets. Examples are 'Christian Ponder would be proud', 'Thank the Gods', political replies and dark jokes.

This is the final version, so I return to version 6, the best measured formulation. Its parts are:
- evaluative-word criteria;
- neutral for logistics, bare invitations, stats, dry headlines and court news;
- explicit neutral carve-outs for tactical sports second-guessing, game recaps and laughed-off hassles;
- mild disappointment markers (:(, bummed, dang it) as negative.

I keep version 6's wording, which was within the word limit.
